  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Displate for supporting PBS. For \na while back there, we thought we might be able  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to avoid the black hole. They'd been lurking as \nshadows in our theories of gravity forever. Enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass crammed into a small enough space would \nlead to a gravitational field from which not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even life could escape from the surrounding \nsurface that we call the event horizon. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon generates paradoxes that worry \nphysicists. And the singularity of infinite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28800,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density within the black hole worries them even \nmore. And so many brave physicists have fought  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for centuries to prove that these monsters \ndon't exist. They hoped nature would step  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in to save us from the theoretical horror \nof ultimate gravitational collapse. One of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our final hopes is the plank star, a ball \nof energy at the heart of the black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54000,
    "dDurationMs": 12720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like frozen shards of the Big Bang. Well, \nlet's hope they're real for physics's sake."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, a couple of quick \nannouncements. First, we have some new data.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liking and commenting really does help get the \nepisodes shared. So, you know, please do both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those things. And we've also learned that \nthe number one reason that people support us on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patreon isn't actually the perks. It's simply to \nsupport the SpaceTime community and the work that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89840,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we do. So, for those of you who do support, thank \nyou so so much. But don't get me wrong, there are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perks and they're good. There's a link in the \ndescription if you'd like to join. This really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be a huge help. Next up, we're excited \nto announce our Fates of the Universe UV Glow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "T-shirt and hoodie. Whether you're rooting for \nthe Big Rip, the Big Crunch, or the Big Freeze,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can wear your prediction for the universe's \nultimate fate. And thanks to the UV glow printing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it lights up after sun exposure, even in the \ndepths of space. Now, on to the episode. First,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there were the dark stars of Mitchell and \nLelass, constructed with only Newtonian gravity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These things were gigantic, 500 times the \nsize of the sun in Mitchell's mind. Happily,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they aren't possible. Giant clouds of gas fragment \nand collapse before a dark star can form. But any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter collapsed far enough will have an event \nhorizon. Those collapsing gas fragments would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form black holes themselves if they were not \nsaved by the onset of nuclear fusion as internal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature and pressure spikes. The resulting \noutflow of energy counters the gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crush, birthing a true star, which saves us from \ncollapse until nuclear fuel runs out. Then the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168400,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse must continue. But happily, not to \na black hole. Not yet. At extreme densities,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think an entire star crammed into the volume of \nthe Earth, new strange quantum effects come to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rescue. The electrons of the stellar core \nare crushed until all available quantum states  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are filled and they cannot be forced together \nanymore. The resulting electron degeneracy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure halts collapse once again, giving us \na white dwarf. Nature seems to have stepped in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200240,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to halt the absurdity of the black hole. So far so \ngood for our hero physicists. We have Mitchell and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lelass getting us into trouble in the first place \nwith dark stars. Then Arthur Edington figured out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that stellar fusion halts collapse and it was \nRalph Fowler applying the brand new field of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics that gave us white dwarfs. \nBut then Subramanion Chandra Seca came along  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and at only 19 years old on an ocean voyage from \nIndia to Cambridge to begin working with Fowler,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he proved that even his new bosses white dwarfs \nhave a failure point. For any white dwarf 40% more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive than the sun, gravitational crush will \nalways exceed the outward electron degeneracy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure. In fact, by including Einstein's \nrelativity to the quantum descriptions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chandra found that outward pressure no longer \nrises fast enough to resist the rising crush  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253440,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a white dwarf gains mass, leading to runaway \ncollapse. Edington was famously very annoyed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this result and disputed it. He was convinced that \nnature must prevent such absurdities as infinite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse. But Chandra was right about the white \ndwarf collapse. Maybe Edington will still be right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the ultimate infinite collapse. There's \none last resppite for collapsing stellar cores  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when physicists realize that electron capture by \nprotons could halt collapse as a neutron star. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the salvation of the stellar core is limited in \nthis case. The more mass of the neutron star, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more compact it becomes. For neutron stars over \na certain mass, the surface gravity stops light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298640,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from escaping and the dreaded event horizon forms. \nAnd that's it. We've lost our battle to stop black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes a long time ago. In fact, they are real. \nWe've seen them in their extreme gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effects across the universe and in gravitational \nwaves and now even in images. They are real. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frankly, I think that's awesome. You might like \nthem, too. I mean, you clicked on this episode.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what's this about physicists wanting to avoid \nthe event horizon? Well, people were uncomfortable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the idea of black holes for good reasons. \nAnd the best reason emerged in the 70s when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steven Hawking and others showed that black \nholes slowly radiate away their mass, shrink,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ultimately vanish. The main problem with this \nis that all information of everything swallowed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole is deleted in that process. This \nviolates a core tenet of quantum mechanics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information conservation. The other problem with \nthe formation of an event horizon is that there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is no known process that can stop matter within it \nfrom collapsing into a point of infinite density  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the center. This singularity generates plenty \nof its own problems. Not least of which is that in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these conditions, the two theories that we use \nto get this far, quantum mechanics and general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity, are so conflicted that they can't \nbe simultaneously true. Black holes point to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental flaws in our theories of nature. Okay, \nso even if we couldn't prevent the event horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe we can at least stop the formation of the \ntheorybreaking singularity. New generations of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists took up the ancient battle to save us \nfrom this theoretical catastrophe. Most believed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the solution must lie in a union of quantum \nmechanics and general relativity. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theory proposed fuzz balls in which matter \nunravels into its stringy weave filling the region  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410640,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beneath the horizon. And we covered that already. \nAnother possible solution is the plank star. a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star of near absolute collapse supported in the \nlast instant only by the grainy structure of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime itself. This comes from what has been \ncalled the main competitor of string theory loop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431520,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum gravity. In LQG, space at the tiniest \nscales is blocky. In particular, it's built up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantiz 2D area elements whose interplay looks \nlike 3D space on larger scales. And here larger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means anything significantly bigger than the plank \nlength around 10 theus 35 m. If LQG is right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it should give us the same spacetime as \ndescribed by general relativity on larger scales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that needs to be true of any quantum gravity \ntheory. But none have been completely worked out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so there's some guesswork in connecting \nthe plank scale to the scale of GR. One way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471680,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do that is using so-called semiclassical gravity \nwhich guesses the pertabbations to the equations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of general relativity as we approach the plank \nscale. And this is how Carlo Revelian co got to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their first picture of the plank star. It actually \ncame from an effort to describe what might happen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the entire universe collapsed like a reverse \nbig bang. As densities become extreme enough,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LQG predicts a semiclassical correction to the \ncosmological equations, the Freriedman equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which an anti-gravity-like effect emerges, \ncausing the collapse to bounce outwards. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loop quantum cosmology is meant to describe an \ninfinitely expanding and contracting universe with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515360,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loopy bounces between cycles. But in 2014, Ralli \nand Franchesco Vidatoto showed how this result  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a collapsing universe could also be used to \napproximate the end result of the collapsing star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside a black hole. At a certain point, outward \nquantum pressure causes it to bounce. In a way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's analogous to the quantum degeneracy pressures \nthat stopped our white dwarf and neutron stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from collapsing further. In that case, it was \nquantum particles unable to occupy the same energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549200,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels. But with the plank star, it's the quantum \nelements of spaceime itself doing the work. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557040,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LQG is right, this could handily stop the wicked \nsingularity from ever forming. For a collapsing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun mass star, the resulting object would be \nabout 1 trillionth of a meter. That's small,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it's not pointlike. It's not a singularity. In \nfact, it's 23 orders of magnitude larger than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577680,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plank scale. So, relatively gigantic. This ultra \ncompressed ball of matter is the plank star that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we've been teasing. The thing about plank stars \nis that they shouldn't last for long, at least in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this semiclassical description given back in 2014. \nThe same space-time pressure that stops collapse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also triggers catastrophic rebound. Now, a similar \nthing happens when the core of a massive star is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "halted by the formation of a neutron star. The \nrest of the star explodes outwards as a supernova.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the case of the plank star, the resulting \nrebound is a white hole. Basically, the opposite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a black hole, the time reversal, space and \nthe energy it contains rushing outwards and an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon that reverses its direction. All of \nthis takes place in about the time it takes light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to cross the size of the plank star, which is a a \ntiny fraction of a second. So, it sounds like our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plank star only exists for an instant after the \nblack hole formation before exploding outwards.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in that case, why do we still see black holes \nout there? And why don't we see the flashes of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme energy expected when a white hole forms? \nMaybe you've seen the film Interstellar and you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember that time close to a black hole event \nhorizon slows down from the point of view those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waiting for Matthew McConnA back on Earth. Hours \ncan translate to years. Well, imagine how strong  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664160,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that gravitational time dilation would be from \ndeep below the event horizon. Strong. A rebounding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
